    "result_text": "Done. All tests green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I did\n\nAdded per-country national-number length validation to the E.164 telephone normalization step.\n\n**One file changed:** `NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java` \u2014 I added the check right after the existing 8\u201315 digit range check, where the normalized `digits` string (country code + national digits) already exists:\n\n```java\n// Per-country national-number length: '+61' needs 9 national digits, '+1' needs 10.\nif (digits.startsWith(\"61\") && digits.length() - 2 != 9\n        || digits.startsWith(\"1\") && digits.length() - 1 != 10) {\n    throw new MissingOwnerFieldsException(List.of(\"telephone\"));\n}\n```\n\nIt reuses the existing `MissingOwnerFieldsException` \u2192 `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler` path, which already maps to **400**, so no new escalation class, no YAML wiring, no new files.\n\n## Why here / structural impact\n\n`NormalizeOwnerTelephone` is a single-method class (WMC_other = 0), and the normalized `digits` value it produces is exactly what the rule needs \u2014 so the check is cohesive with that step and cheap by the cost metric: **one file**, one already-simple function, a handful of added lines. A separate `Validate\u2026` step would have doubled the files touched (new class + YAML edit) for no correctness gain.\n\n## Note on the given test\n\n`\"+61 123\"` \u2192 5 digits, which the pre-existing `< 8` check already rejected, so that test passed before. My change adds the *actual* spec behavior: e.g. `+61 12345678` (10 total digits, only 8 national) is now correctly rejected, while the valid `04XXXXXXXX` fixtures (\u2192 `+61` with 9 national digits) and `+1` NANP seed data (10 national digits) still pass.",
